{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>If you take your fellow's garment in pledge, you shall return it to him before the sun goes down.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat) the verse speaks of a garment for the day. And by way of the Midrash: 'If you take your fellow's garment in pledge' — this is what the verse says (Jeremiah 6): ״כסף נמאס קראו להם״ — 'rejected silver they call them.' You find that when Israel went into exile from Jerusalem, He brought them out in iron collars, and the enemies said: the Holy One, blessed be He, has already rejected them, for He is their Elohim, and He said of them 'rejected silver.' Just as silver is refined and made into a vessel, then refined again and made into another vessel, and in the end it reverts to earth and is no longer fit for any work — so Israel have no restoration, for their Elohim has already rejected them. When Jeremiah heard this, he said (Jeremiah 14): ״המאס מאסת את יהודה״ — 'Have You utterly rejected Judah?' This is likened to a king who struck his wife. The matchmaker said to him: 'If you seek to divorce her, strike her until she dies; and if you intend to turn your heart back to her, why deal so strictly with her?' The king said to him: 'Even if my palaces lie in ruins, I do not divorce her.' So Jeremiah said to the Holy One, blessed be He: 'If You wish to divorce us, strike us until You obliterate our memory, as it is written (Lamentations 5): ״כי אם מאס מאסתנו קצפת עלינו עד מאד״; and if not, (Jeremiah 14): ״מדוע הכיתנו ואין לנו מרפא״ — why have You stricken us, and there is no healing for us?' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'At the time that I destroy My world, I divorce Israel, as it is said (Jeremiah 31): ״כה אמר ה' אם ימדו שמים מלמעלה ויחקרו מוסדי ארץ למטה גם אני אמאס בכל זרע ישראל״ — thus says YHWH: if the heavens above can be measured and the foundations of the earth below searched out, then I too will reject all the seed of Israel. Rather, even so I have stipulated with them that if they sin, the Temple will be held in pledge on their account, as it is said (Leviticus 26): ״ונתתי משכני בתוככם״ — do not read משכני (My dwelling) but משכוני (My pledge). And likewise Balaam said (Numbers 24): ״מה טובו אהליך יעקב משכנותיך ישראל״ — 'how goodly are your tents, O Jacob, your dwellings, O Israel' — your dwellings when they are ruins. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: Not because I love the nations do I pledge My sanctuary for you, but your sins caused Me to do so, as it is said (Isaiah 50): ״אי זה ספר כריתות אמכם אשר שלחתיה״ — 'where is the bill of divorce of your mother, whom I sent away?' — ״הן בעונותיכם נמכרתם ובפשעיכם שלחה אמכם״ — 'behold, for your transgressions you were sold, and for your rebellions your mother was sent away.' 'If you take your fellow's garment in pledge' — when Israel transgress the commandments, I pledge two pledges. And until when? 'Before the sun goes down' — until the Messiah comes, of whom it is written (Malachi 3): ״וזרחה לכם יראי שמי שמש צדקה מרפא בכנפיה״ — 'unto you who fear My name shall the sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings.' And it is written (Psalms 72): ״לפני שמש ינון שמו״ — 'before the sun his name endures.' 'In that hour you shall return it to him.' Thus far in the Midrash.\n\nAnd know that Israel are called ריעים (fellows/companions) to the Omnipresent, as it is written (Psalms 122): ״למען אחי ורעי״. And He likened the Temple to a שלמה (garment) on account of the whiteness of the שלמה, for it is white and bright; and so King Solomon, peace be upon him, called it and likened it to curtains, as it is written (Song of Songs 1): ״כיריעות שלמה״. And Moses our teacher mentioned it by the name לבנון, as it is written (Deuteronomy 3): ״ההר הטוב הזה והלבנון״, and Onkelos translated it as בית מקדשא — the Temple."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:28.306562652Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}